<!--Post: 8133--><!--Time: 1108921432-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8133"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 20, 2005 - 9:43 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I am pretty sure that BTK&#39;s monster, if not the result of, is aggravated by drug use, due to his violent nature and some written clues&#40;perta&amp;tina&#41;. If I was understanding correctly,  tina is slang for methedrine, meaning: If petra=rock&#43;tina=meth, then petra/tina could be crystal methedrine [or if] petra=petrol=fuel&#43;tina=meth, then petra/tina could mean methedrine/fueled. <BR> <BR>If BTK is using meth, and he doesn&#39;t have his own lab, then he is buying it somewhere. <BR> <BR> <BR>Due to his spooky notoriety, I would think that he would be pretty obvious to any local dealer and they would put two and two together and figure out who he is. <BR> <BR>If they knew he was BTK they would 1&#41;worry that he would kill their wife/girlfrind or daughter. 2&#41;Be worried that he would draw them, somehow into a murder conviction because they were supplying him. 3&#41; would just be scared s---less of him because he&#39;s nuts and might kill them. <BR> <BR>At any rate they would probably make an annonomous call and turn him in just so they could sleep with both eyes closed. <BR> <BR>I suspect that he has a place, possibly where he has some family or old aquaintances, where, If he is using regularly, he buys drugs in a substancial amount. This may be a &#39;hole in the wall&#39; so to speak, where he might keep up a normal front with the idea that if all hell breaks loose, he can slip away and blend into the scenery. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m just guessing, but as I surf through different clues, the areas that pop up are: <BR> <BR>The area north of KC where Neb,Kan,Mo,and Iowa meet; The Kan,Mo,Ark,Ok corner near Tulsa; and particularly the Kan,Mo border&#43;central Mo around the northern edge of the Ozark Plateau. &#40;I would say KC, but I think he would avoid a large city that he didn&#39;t know already&#41; <BR> <BR>I think in one of these areas there is a drug dealer who should recognize him if he was alerted to the fact that he/she might be dealing with BTK. <BR> <BR>I can&#39;t imagine even the toughest, meanest, greediest drug dealer wanting to deal with a person of BTK&#39;s nature if they knew who he was.  <BR> <BR>If the authorities were to spread the word...they might be surprised how quickly he shows up. After all, who pays closer attention to who their customers are than drug dealers? <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8133-->
<!--Post: 8149--><!--Time: 1108930889-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8149"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Audreyamw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Audreyamw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 20, 2005 - 12:21 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I haven&#39;t been able to find any info on SK and their histories of recreational drug use, or how common it is among SK.  Still looking around for info.   <BR> <BR>Perhaps BTK is or has been involved in dealing, transporting drugs, or something similar.  Paul Bernardo is a SK who liked to supplement his income by smuggling stolen goods and cigarettes in large quantities.  The WPD investigated a possible connection between BTK and drug dealing in the 1970s because of some homicides in Wichita at that time that seemed to be connected to some deals gone bad -- but LE hasn&#39;t speculated about a drug connection recently.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8149-->
<!--Post: 8164--><!--Time: 1108942973-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8164"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsbarn<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsbarn<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 20, 2005 - 3:42 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->From Sexual Homicide: Patterns and Motives &#40;1992&#41; Ressler, Burgess and Douglas &#40;a statistical study of linking characteristics among sexual offenders&#41;: <BR> <BR><i>Although some of the murderers in our study did not report fantasies in a conscious way, their descriptions of the murders they committed reveal hidden fantasies of violence, often against women. Several of the men decribed the factors that triggered the killing as their not feeling well, depression, alcohol or drugs. Out of 118 victim cases half &#40;49 percent&#41; of the murderers said that they had been drinking prior to the crime. The murderers were asked if their use of alcohol at the time of the offense differed significantly from typical drinking. Thirty percent said yes. Drug use at the time of the offense was 35 percent; however, only 12.5 percent said that drug use differed significantly from their typical drug habits... <BR> <BR>...we suspect that these murderers are preoccupied with a kind of internal dialogue that sustains anger, discontent, irritability, or depression. Use of alcohol or drugs is an attempt to moderate such internal stress, but the fantasy continues.</i> <BR> <BR>By examining the &#34;gateway&#34; phenomenon among adolescents &#40;those who smoke are more likely to move on to alcohol, those who drink are more likely to move on drugs&#41; and applying that to a sociopathic adolescent, then there would be a chain of delinquent behavior leading up to the murders that BTK committed as an adult. Given that, I think it is likely, that after careful planning and preparation encased in the fantasy, BTK needed something to dull the fear of following through and drugs and/or alcohol may have provided a familiar catalyst to follow through with the plan, at least that makes sense to me. <BR> <BR>To quote a line from a song by the Drive By Truckers, &#34;You know the bottle ain&#39;t to blame and I ain&#39;t trying to; It don&#39;t make you do a thing it just lets you,&#34; BTK may have had some need for drugs or alcohol to conduct the murders.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8164-->
<!--Post: 8169--><!--Time: 1108946585-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8169"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 20, 2005 - 4:43 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Don&#39;t bother trying to classify BTK as a &#34;SK&#34;. He will imitate every serial killer ever written about as far as it suits his purpose. Other than that he will do as he pleases. <BR> <BR>Nothing I have read about him or read from him has given me any reason to that, if he is mentally ill, he is compulsive to the point that he he is particularly predictable if he doesn&#39;t want to be.  <BR> <BR>I agree for the most part with Michael Kelleher&#39;s profile of BTK, and can accept on a metephorical level, BTK&#39;s biography. I don&#39;t think that he was an abused child as much as I think he was affected by a rough adolecence and probably drugs. <BR> <BR>I do think that the &#34;fiddler grandfather&#34; has some basis in fact as well as &#34;being in the military&#34;, however I see him maybe in more of a &#34;paramilitary soldier of fortune&#34; outfit in Missouri or Tennessee around the Rockabilly era. <BR> <BR>I think when his father died or left, his mother <BR>started &#34;dating&#34; a guy who he didn&#39;t take seriously as an authority figure, and the combination of loss of his father, realization of his mother&#39;s sexuality, hatred of the new guy, and normal rebellion occurred after a relatively normal childhood, but before he matured.  <BR> <BR>It would also all have occurred at the time just prior and during the time when everyone was &#34;tuning in, turning on and dropping out&#34;. Having a lot of anger he probably leaned to the tougher fringe of the &#34;revolution&#34;. <BR> <BR>Part of him just never came back all the way and he cloaked it, learned he could, thereby nurturing the evil side subconciously into what he calls his monster. <BR> <BR>he might even actually believe that the monster controls him. But I think he can see, as well as anyone, that the monster has to be fed before the monster appears. <BR> <BR>BTK likes control. and he knows that he is going to lose what he has now, one way or another. He has to give up something and he knows it. he can give up the book, dissappear and maybe stay hidden the rest of his life. He can go until he is caught or killed, but if he is jailed against his will, the monster will take him permanently. he can commit suicide and be done with it all. Or he can surrender himself and spend the rest of his life instutionalized, writing his book, probably even a sequal, talking to shrinks, and basically being a minor celebrity to a few. But to do that, he still has to beat the monster first.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
